  "strategy": "We first initialize the optimization with the parent solution (coefficients) if provided.\n        If the parent scores worse than a randomly generated one, we fall back.\n\n        Specifically, our steps are:\n\n        a) Read input: current grid size (n) or force use parent if available.\n        b) Transition to coefficient space with the Chebyshev transform.\n        c) Define optimization routine with adaptive constraint scaling.\n        d) Use a primal-dual interior-point method to track progress.\n        e) Iteratively shrink dual variables to enforce tight integration.\n        f) Use gradient-based updates with L-BFGS-B to maintain sparse solutions.\n\n        g) Repair subroutines:\n            - Reset coefficients if solution leaves feasible set.\n\n        We use dual-variables to enforce L1 constraints and coef reductions:\n\n            l = cgression_matrix\n            dual_step = min(step, 100)   # adaptive step control\n\n        Alternative formulations if the SDP becomes too large use iterative refinement with compressed sensing bases.\n\n    </implementation-details>\n\n    Feasibility transfer algorithm (to match grid to basis):\n\n        def transfer_parent(parent_np_array, new_n):\n            # Interpolate parent array to new_n points\n            x_old = np.linspace(0, 2, parent_np_array.size)\n            x_new = np.linspace(0, 2, new_n)\n            parent_new = np.interp(x_new, x_old, parent_np_array)\n            return parent_new\n\n        This is valid because we assume parent is continuous and can be arbitrarily interpolated.\n\n    We hope this approach outperforms the current record by attacking the problem at its harmonic core.",
